WayNet: bound the waypoint and edge counts, which size a reserve before anything is read.

`WayNet::load` takes both of its counts straight off the file and validates
neither:

	auto count_points = r.read_int();  // numWaypoints
	this->points.reserve(count_points);
	...
	auto count_edges = r.read_int();   // numWays
	this->edges.reserve(count_edges);

Same shape as the mesh element counts patch 0036 bounds, and the edge loop has
the same reason it cannot fail on its own: `ReadArchive::read_object` past the
end of the entry stream logs "Expected object, got entry" and returns **null**
rather than throwing, and the loop tolerates a null endpoint by design (patch
0033 -- a reference into a waynet ZenGin itself wrote can go unresolved). So it
runs to the file's own count over reads that give nothing back, pushing two null
points and one edge per iteration on top of the memory `reserve` has already
committed.

Measured on the unpatched reader against `zenkit-node`'s 1.4 KB fixture world
with `numWays` rewritten in place to 0x0FFFFFFF: 268 million edges and 536
million null waypoints, and the world still reports **LOADED**, after 41 s. That
is the dangerous case -- an *absurd* count is the harmless one, because
`reserve` throws `bad_alloc` and the loop is never entered.

The waypoint count is bounded by the same argument even though its loop already
fails loudly: patch 0033's null guard throws on the first waypoint that is not
there, but only after `reserve` has committed 4.3 GB.

Both counts are bounded by the bytes actually left in the reader, with the same
`_bytes_remaining` helper as `Mesh.cc` and `BspTree.cc`. The per-object floor is
four bytes, under the smallest header any of the three archive formats can
write, because a bound must never refuse a world the engine loads.

Found by construction rather than by luck: `zenkit-node/tools/fuzz-world.js
--counts` sweeps every INTEGER entry in the stream in turn. 600 random seeds
over the fixture and 160 over retail NewWorld never found it -- 20 to 500 random
byte writes have no reason to land on the four bytes of a count.

